WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.280
Do Vegans Have Lower Bone
Density and More Fractures?

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:05.600
This video and the next explore
whether there's a difference

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.977
in bone density between those
who eat meat and those who don't.

00:00:08.977 --> 00:00:18.795
"Bone Density, Fracture Rates,
and Vitamin D in Vegans"

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.760
Osteoporosis has become a major
public health problem worldwide.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.520
The morbidity and even mortality
of osteoporotic complications,

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:30.320
such as hip fractures, are severe.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.800
Osteoporosis is diagnosed by testing
low in bone mineral density

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:41.120
and afflicts about 1 in 20 men
over age 65, and 1 in four women.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.680
"Do We Need to Be Concerned
about Bone Mineral Density

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.080
in Vegetarians and Vegans? "

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.120
There are studies showing
vegetarian-style diets during

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:52.800
adolescence can have a positive
impact on bone in young adulthood,

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.040
but what we really want to know is
about osteoporosis at older ages.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:00.800
In an earlier video, I talked about
a meta-analysis that concluded

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.160
that vegetarian diets, particularly
vegan diets, were associated

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.400
with lower bone mineral density,

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:09.600
but only by a clinically
insignificant amount.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.880
Given the relationship between fracture
risk and bone mineral density,

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:16.720
the relative risk of fracture in vegans
would only be about 10% higher

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:22.320
than in meat-eaters, but that doesn't
sound very insignificant to me.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.960
Now I talked about how the
differences in bone mineral density

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.720
are largely just a function of
vegetarians and particularly vegans

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.280
having such low rates of obesity.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.240
Obese individuals are protected
from osteoporosis because

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.480
they do so much
weight-bearing exercise

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.680
just walking from one room
to the next basically.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:41.920
But we only care about
bone mineral density

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.200
because we care
about bone fractures.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.560
What's the comparative
fracture risk in vegetarians

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:52.800
versus nonvegetarians?
Now we're talking.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.680
Compared with meat eaters,
same risk for vegetarians,

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.160
but a 30% higher risk for vegans.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.040
Now it was mostly wrist
and arm fractures;

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:02.800
there weren't any hip fractures.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:06.320
Wrist fractures are among the most
common fractures, and interestingly

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.360
occurs typically in women who
are in good health and active.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:12.480
It's the kind of fracture you get
if you like trip when you run

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.880
and fall on an outstretched hand.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.440
But the 30% increased risk was after
controlling for non-dietary factors

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.040
including activity, such as
exercise or an active workplace.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:28.480
The increased risk only disappeared
when they controlled for calcium.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:32.720
Vegans only were at higher risk
when they got under 525 mg

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.840
of calcium a day, which is equal to
the estimated average requirement.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.240
Among those getting at least 525
there was no greater risk.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.720
So the higher fracture risk in
the vegans appeared to be

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:47.440
a consequence of
inadequate calcium intake,

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:48.960
which is essential
for bone health

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.600
regardless of what
kind of diet you eat.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.200
You don't need to drink milk.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.120
A greater intake of milk
and dairy products

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:58.960
is not associated with a lower risk
of osteoporosis or hip fracture.

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:03.440
In fact, every additional cup or so
of cow's milk a day was associated

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.080
with a 9% greater risk of hip
fracture in prospective studies.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:10.880
But you do have to get
calcium from somewhere.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:14.240
Plant-based sources include
almonds, sesame seeds, tofu,

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:17.680
calcium-fortified plant milks,
or the best sources:

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:20.800
dark green leafy vegetables
such as kale ---

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.680
basically any dark green leafies,
except for spinach, beet greens,

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.800
or chard, which are just
stingy with their calcium.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:30.640
And most vegans in the study
were getting more than the 525.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.520
There's lots of healthy foods
packed with calcium,

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:37.280
but they only work if
you actually eat them.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:38.000
But wait.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.200
What about the mountain of data
showing that dietary calcium intake

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.840
is not associated with risk
of fractures, and there's

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:47.360
no evidence that increasing calcium
intake prevents fractures,

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:50.080
and so increasing calcium intake
should not be recommended

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.160
for fracture prevention.

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:55.200
But that's based on giving
extra calcium to people

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.760
who are already getting
enough calcium.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.400
So it might just be like
a plateau effect.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:04.480
Take women getting only 500 mg of
calcium a day and randomize them

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:05.760
to calcium supplements,

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.480
and you can drop hip fracture
rates 40% within 18 months.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:13.520
Now they also gave them vitamin D,
and the women did start out

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:16.880
seriously deficient with vitamin D
levels down around 15,

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:20.960
so it's hard to tease out the
effects of calcium versus the D.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:24.880
But vegans who aren't supplementing
with D at higher latitudes

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:28.720
can dip down that low
during the winter months, too.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:31.920
Now there was a study in Shanghai
that found comparable bone health,

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:34.960
despite lower D levels
down around 15.

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:36.560
They were also low
in calcium intake

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.200
and still had similar
bone mineral density,

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:42.560
but given that fracture study,
I'd recommend people make sure

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:45.920
they're getting enough calcium
and enough vitamin D.

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:49.520
But that fracture study
was published in 2007.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.880
A 2020 update found
a higher risk of fractures,

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:57.520
even in vegans getting more
than 700 mg of calcium a day.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:59.040
What explains that?

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:03.360
We'll explore just that question next.

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:06.640
As I noted in my video on
bone mineral density,

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:10.960
vegetarians had slightly lower bone
mineral density in their spines.

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:13.920
Although the difference was
basically within the margin of error

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:15.840
for the test, if the bone quality

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.880
really is compromised, it could
lead to collapsed vertebrae,

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:22.400
increased spinal fracture risk,
but there's no evidence for this.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:25.520
The incidence of vertebral fracture
was ascertained in older women

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:30.480
who had been vegan for most of
their lives -- 34 years on average.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:34.480
And despite their calcium intake
being terrible, about half that

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:38.240
of the nonvegans, and a quarter
of them vitamin D deficient,

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:42.320
the incidence of vertebral fractures
was not significantly different.

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:45.040
Although the vegans had a higher
prevalence of vitamin D deficiency

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:48.320
and lower dietary calcium
intakes, the two factors

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:50.480
were not associated
with bone loss.

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:53.520
In fact, the annual loss in bone
mineral density in the hips

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:56.880
of vegans was less than
half that of the meat eaters,

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.640
though the difference did not
reach statistical significance.

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:02.880
Vegetarian women had not been
found to be at higher risk

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:06.240
of any kind of fractures including
wrist fractures in this case,

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:09.600
though among vegetarians,
those who consumed the least

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:12.960
vegetable protein intake were
at the highest risk for fracture.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:16.320
Those who ate beans every day,
or nuts, or like veggie burgers

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:19.360
only had a third of the wrist fractures
compared to vegetarians

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:21.920
who only ate beans or
other higher protein foods

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:23.840
less than three times a week.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:26.880
So those that consume a vegan
or vegetarian diet may be

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:30.560
at increased risk of fracture unless
care is taken to ensure an adequate

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:34.800
quantity and variety of foods high
in protein, such as whole grains,

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:38.800
nuts, and beans, split peas,
chickpeas, or lentils are in the diet.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:41.520
That's one of the reasons
in my free Daily Dozen app

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:45.200
I recommend whole grains
and legumes every day.

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:48.480
Hip fractures are even more
serious. Those eating legumes,

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:51.120
like beans every day, reduced
their risk of hip fracture

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:56.400
by more than 60%, compared to
40% lower risk from meat protein,

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:59.680
with plant-based meats
coming in in between

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:03.120
with about 50% lower
risk of hip fractures.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:05.440
What's the bottom line
on plant-based diets and

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:07.680
bone health according
to this 2020 review?

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:11.200
Theoretically, a long-term
plant-based diet may reduce

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:15.040
the risk of osteoporosis, but
that has yet to be demonstrated.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:18.080
What we do know is that
plant-based diets, when ensuring

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:20.080
adequate calcium and vitamin D levels,

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:24.160
don't appear to have any
detrimental effects on bone health.

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:27.040
But this was published August 2020.

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:30.800
In November 2020, the 12-year
follow-up to the study I talked about

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:34.080
in my last video on comparative
fracture risk in vegetarians

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:35.280
versus nonvegetarians

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:38.880
was published, finding that
non-meat eaters, especially vegans,

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:41.040
had higher risks of
total bone fractures,

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:45.120
including at sites associated with
osteoporosis such as hip fractures.

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:48.800
It comes out to be about 20 more
cases in vegans for every 1000

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:50.160
people over 10 years.

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:54.560
So if indeed this is cause-and-effect,
eating vegan there would be

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:57.600
an annual 1 in 500 chance
of having a bone fracture

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:00.080
that you otherwise
might not have had.

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:02.320
Was it because they weren't
eating enough beans?

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:04.800
Apparently not, since
vegans getting more protein

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:07.040
still apparently had higher risk.

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:10.240
Maybe it was because they
weren't getting enough calcium?

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:12.560
Apparently not, since vegans
getting more calcium

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:15.360
still apparently had higher risk.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:20.000
What about bone and vitamin B12?
If you remember EPIC-Oxford,

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:21.920
where the bone data comes from,

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:26.480
is the same group of British vegans
who had rampant B12 deficiency.

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:30.160
More than half the vegans were
B12 deficient because they

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:34.560
weren't adequately supplementing
with B12 or B12-fortified foods.

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:37.280
This can lead to high
homocysteine levels,

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:39.200
which not only increase stroke risk,

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:42.960
but may increase the
activity of bone-eating cells.

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:47.360
This was in a petri dish, but you do
indeed see low bone mineral density

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:50.720
in those born with a birth defect
that leads to high homocysteine

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:52.960
levels in the blood. Therefore,

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:56.000
high serum homocysteine
may be regarded as a factor

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.720
that can reduce both
bone mass and quality,

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:03.040
but you don't know
until you put it to the test.

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:06.720
And homocysteine-lowering
treatment failed to reduce

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:10.320
the risk of bone fracture.
So in the end, the effect of B12

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:15.200
deficiency in bone health
remains to be established.

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:19.920
OK, so how do we explain the higher
fracture rates found among vegans?

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:22.960
The investigators conclude that their
findings suggest that bone health

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:27.280
in vegans requires further research,
but there were some clues.

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:30.000
The elevated fracture risk,
both for total fractures

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.160
and for hip fractures specifically,

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:36.640
was only significant for
those under a BMI of 22.5,

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:40.640
which is like under about 130 pounds
for a woman of average height.

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:45.120
So part of the problem is that vegans
tend to be so slender on average.

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:48.640
Why are overweight and obese
individuals protected from fractures?

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:52.800
Well, think about it. They have
cushioning during a fall.

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:54.560
There's more of a
cushion on your hips.

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:58.240
Also, there's an enzyme in fatty
tissue that churns out estrogen,

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:00.880
which is why women increase
their breast cancer risk

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:04.960
a percentage point for every
pound they gain in adulthood.

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:08.000
But estrogen can also have
a bone-preserving effect.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:11.440
Now you can get the best of both
worlds consuming soy foods,

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:14.400
preventing bone loss while
at the same time associated

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:17.520
with lessening breast
cancer risk for both estrogen

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:20.880
receptor positive tumors and
estrogen receptor negative tumors.

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:24.240
Finally, overweight and obese
individuals may also have

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:27.200
stronger bones just from
the increased weight-bearing.

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:29.920
Carrying 100 extra pounds,
you're doing major

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:33.200
weight bearing exercise
just walking across the room.

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:36.560
So the risk difference they saw
between vegans and meat eaters

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:41.040
were likely at least partially
due to the difference in BMI.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:44.720
My money, however,
is on vitamin D.

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:48.640
Great Britain is at
Canadian latitudes.

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:50.400
The sun's rays are
at such an angle

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:53.440
during the winter months up there
that the vitamin D levels among

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:58.080
British vegans in the wintertime
drops down to suboptimal levels.

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:01.040
Ideally, we should be up around
75 nanomoles per liter,

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:04.960
or 30 nanograms per milliliter
depending on what units you're using,

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:10.160
which the vegans nail in the summer.
It is the sunshine vitamin after all.

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:13.600
But in the winter, not getting
the vitamin D added to dairy or

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:17.840
found naturally in oily fish, if vegans
aren't supplementing at that latitude

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:21.760
during the winter, their vitamin
D levels may drop too low.

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:25.200
Now randomized controlled trials
show that vitamin D alone

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:27.360
does not seem to
reduce fracture rates,

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:31.600
but boosting people's D and
calcium at the same time does.

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:35.760
So maybe it was a combination of the
relatively low D and calcium intakes

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:38.800
among the vegans that led
to their higher fracture rates.

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:41.920
We won't know for sure
until it's actually put to the test,

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:45.714
and when it is you can be sure
I'll do a video about it.

